A||N||22||Monkeyman||Just some questions...||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||06-03-2000||03:05 PM||||I finally got a chance to take the Grape Ape up to highway speeds.  Wow!!  <P>Does 2600rpms @ 60mph sound about right?<P>I have a slight shimmy in the steering wheel at about the same speed.  Alignment?  Or something far worse?  The local mechanic said that the suspension and steering seemed OK.  At least nothing major that time didn't cause.  I know that I have the original shocks and struts.  Could this cause the shimmy?<P>For those of you who don't know the Grape Ape, she's an '85 GT, automatic.||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||06-03-2000||03:36 PM||||Glad to hear you are back in the saddle Monkey. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>As far as the RPM thing goes, I show about 2200 @ 60 mph in 4th gear. I don't know if this is because its a fresh engine, or because of the modifications, or just the tranny. <BR>I have (from in to out) a K&N Filtercharger, Accell super coil & 8mm spiral core wires, Bosch plugs, 2.5" exhaust, low restriction Random Technologies cat and Flowmaster muffler. Only 5000 miles on rebuild w/ .030 overbore. <BR>All that stuff and only 400 rpm difference? Sounds like you have a pretty strong motor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick's Dad||06-03-2000||08:28 PM||||Hi Monk,<P>Shimmy sounds to me to be:<P>1)Tire balance. Usually doesn't manifest itself until highway speeds. Often, the wheel in question will jump up and down, like it wants to leave the axle.<P>2)Lower ball joints. This is the most dangerous of them all. This will get progressively worse, but you don't want to push them. Watch also for cupping and uneven wear on the insides of the tires. Uppers are just generally vague feeling that actually impove temporarily when you push them. You don't want to.... When a bj fails, the wheel will fold itself into the wheel well. You don't want that happening at 25, much less 65.<P>3)Steering rack. Do you "bump steer?" That is, does your car change directions when you hit a bump? Does it follow ruts in the road? I changed mine last year with TFS's rebuilt, and it made a world of difference.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Big O||06-03-2000||08:36 PM||||Just another thing to look for, if you have aftermarket wheels, you may need to invest in some hub-centric spacers.  These are rings you slip between the wheel and the hubs to center the wheel.  Sometimes using the wheel studs to center the wheel on the hub isn't precise enough.  I have this problem on my 86 GT.  I own a tire store and I've had my guys go through my suspension system and alignment twice and I still have a slight shimmy at speed that feels like the rack in the rack and pinion is worn (it isn't).  I've got Sendel wheels (16") all around.  They aren't the greatest quality wheel and I've ordered a set of hub-centric rings to see if this will take care of the problem.  By the way, my 3.8 Series II SC cruises at 80 mph @1900 rpm.||||reg||3
Z||000004||Monkeyman||06-03-2000||11:20 PM||||stimpy--I have an automatic so that might make up the 400rpm difference.<P>Patrick's Dad--1)  When I get back from Ohio next weekend, I'll get the wheels balanced just to be sure.<P>2)  I hadn't planned on rebuilding the front suspension until early next spring.  How can I tell if this is the problem?  Can a tire/alignment shop tell or do I just have to change them just to be safe?<P>3)  No bump steer.  Tracks great for a 15 year old car with the original everything.<P>Big )--How much do they cost and where would I get them?||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||06-04-2000||02:45 AM||||MM, look and se what (if any) wear there is on the tires -- I had both fronts cupped bad around the inside edges due to bad shocks (they were the original shocks on an '87GT!!!) -- The alignment was perfect and the tire balancing was right and the steering stabilizer was ok - I changed the shocks and solved the shimmy at speed problem.<P>Also, I have an automatic too and that RPM at 60 mph is pretty darn close to my '87GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||sdm1964||06-04-2000||05:45 AM||||Glad to see you got it running again. My car revs at that speed too.||||reg||14
Z||000007||batboy||06-04-2000||09:06 AM||||My friend had a bad front end shimmy at between 50-60 MPH, it turned out to be a bad tire on front. When you have them balanced, try rotating the front wheels to the back and see what happens. To check for front suspension problems, grab the front tires in the 3 and 9 o'clock postions and sharply try to yank it back and forth. Any extra play should be obvious and could be worn tie rods, wheel bearings, or steering rack. Now with the wheel jacked up, do the same with your hands in the 12 and 6 o'clock position to check the ball joints (especially look for movement in the lowers). It's helpful to have assistance on this one so you can actually watch the ball joints. I've also used a crowbar and carefully pried around the ball joints looking for movement. Maybe this will help ya out a little.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||06-04-2000||10:49 AM||||Thanx everybuggy.  I don't think anything's wrong with the tires.  They are almost brand new Pirellis.  I found a tire shop that will check my suspension/steering for free.  I'll try to get in there after work tomorrow.  Hopefully there won't be anything serious, but if there is, I have permission from the boss (no, not Bruce Springsteen) to get it fixed.  I had planned on completely rebuilding the suspension early next year.  Maybe I'll have to do it sooner.  Thanx for the help.  Let's see if I have the possible problems right.  Bad tires (not likely), worn shocks (just about guaranteed), bad ball joints (hope not) or those spacer things.||||reg||1
Z||000009||phillip||06-04-2000||10:59 AM||||guess i'm kinda late on input, but i'll throw in a vote for tire balance too. can you rotate tires to see if it changes or do you have "spare wheels" you can try?<BR>it's a cheap place to start. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||06-04-2000||11:57 AM||||I can rotate the wheels, but I don't have any other rims to put on.  If the tire shop can't find anything else wrong with the suspension/steering, I'll probably just have all 4 tires rebalanced.||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||06-04-2000||12:59 PM||||Tires will take about a week of driving to seat onto the rim completely,then usually need a rebalance at that time I've noticed.<BR>I had a set of tires shaved in order to get them to be true once,worked great;triple digit speeds and not the slightest hint of shimmy.Tires actually wear better after this has been done.<BR>Shocks will make a difference also,if they're not working.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Big O||06-04-2000||10:06 PM||||The hub centric rings run about $5.00 per set (wholesale), you might have to pay twice that retail.  Check with your local tire/wheel distributor for them.  Be sure they get you a set that fits your application of car and wheel.  <P>Another thing you need to look for.  I have a new piece of equipment in my shop from Hunter Engineering.  It's a road force measurement wheel balancer.  They are new and not many shops have them.  The principle of the machine is to apply a force to the tire to look for out of round conditions, hard spots in the tire, and damaged wheels.  You can balance a wheel perfectly and if any of these conditions exist you will get a ride disturbance.  This is true even if you have new tires.  Hard spots in the tread are caused during manufacture of the tire.  The road force measurement will find the spot and tell you if it's possible to match mount the tire to the wheel to bring it within OEM specs.  If it can't be match mounted and the problem is hard spots, return the tires under the manufacturers warranty against defects.  You can balance a square block to perfection, but you'll never get it to run smooth.||||reg||1
Z||000013||flexableface||06-04-2000||10:20 PM||||i was wondering how much a new or rebuilt steering rack would cost and how hard is it to replace. Also do i need to get an alingment when i put the new steering rack on and where can i get one from.   <BR>      thanks luke Simpson<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Big O||06-05-2000||10:39 AM||||I'm not sure how much racks run in your area, but here in Modesto CA they are around $300.00 for a rebuilt unit.  Installation and alignment is additional.  You will need to have the front end realigned after installing the new unit just to be sure everything is where it's supposed to be.  Front end alignments are cheap insurance for your tires.||||reg||1
